window.EXAM_DATA = {"id":"cca-practice-5","title":"실전 모의고사 5","subtitle":"Practice Exam 5 · 60 Questions · D1→D5 순서","kind":"mock","timeLimitSec":7200,"passScaled":720,"domains":{"1":"Agentic Architecture & Orchestration","2":"Tool Design & MCP Integration","3":"Claude Code Configuration & Workflows","4":"Prompt Engineering & Structured Output","5":"Context Management & Reliability"},"domainWeights":{"1":27,"2":18,"3":20,"4":20,"5":15},"domainShort":{"1":"Agentic","2":"Tools & MCP","3":"Claude Code","4":"Prompting","5":"Context"},"note":"자체 제작 CCAR-F 연습 문제입니다(비공식). 문제·보기는 영어, 해설은 한국어. 이 도구의 점수식은 공식 채점과 다릅니다.","questions":[{"domain":1,"stem":"A Code Generation team has a task: rename a variable consistently across a codebase. Which architectural class fits BEST?","options":["An agent that decides which occurrences to rename based on context","A deterministic workflow with find-and-replace as a fixed step","A conversational chatbot that asks the developer about each occurrence","A multi-agent system with one agent per file"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 세 가지 아키텍처 클래스와 선택 기준\n\n클래스 — 언제 적합한가 — 특징\nConversational — 응답만 필요 (도구·상태 변경 없음) — 사용자 턴에 응답\nWorkflow — 단계가 미리 정해져 있음 (도구를 써도 순서가 고정) — 결정론적, 저비용, 낮은 분산\nAgent — 런타임에 판단/적응이 필요 — 높은 유연성, 높은 비용·분산\n\n- 판별 기준은 \"런타임 의사결정이 필요한가\"입니다. 모든 참조를 찾아 모두 바꾸는 작업은 판단이 없으므로 워크플로입니다.\n\n오답 이유\n- A: 판단 로직을 넣으면 변동성이 생겨 누락이 생길 수 있음. 결정론적 find-replace가 더 안전합니다.\n- C: 발생 건마다 묻는 방식은 대형 코드베이스에서 비현실적입니다.\n- D: 파일당 에이전트는 조율 비용만 늘립니다. 멀티 에이전트는 병렬성 같은 이점이 있을 때 정당화됩니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nConsistent rename across a codebase is a deterministic operation — find every reference, replace it. Tooling exists for this and a workflow wraps the tool. No runtime decisions are required; agentic adaptation has no role.\n(A) The requirement is consistent rename across all occurrences — there is no decision about which to rename. Adding agentic logic introduces variance that could miss occurrences. (C) Per-occurrence prompting is unworkable for any non-trivial codebase. (D) Per-file agents add coordination cost without benefit.","id":"practice-5-1"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research team has a workflow that retrieves and summarizes news on five topics every morning. Stakeholders want to occasionally add new topics that are not in the predetermined set. Which architectural change is MOST consistent with CCA-F guidance?","options":["Replace the workflow with a conversational chatbot that takes topic requests","Replace the workflow entirely with an agent so it can go off and discover brand-new topics entirely on its own at runtime","Run the workflow five times in parallel for the original topics and add a new workflow for new topics","Add a parameter to the workflow so the topic list can be configured per run, keeping the workflow class"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 최소 복잡도 원칙 (minimum-complexity change)\n- \"입력이 달라지는 것\"과 \"런타임 적응이 필요한 것\"은 다릅니다. 입력만 변하면 파라미터화로 충분합니다.\n- 에이전트로의 승격은 실행 중에 경로를 바꿔야 할 때만 정당화됩니다.\n\n오답 이유\n- A: 정해진 시간에 자동으로 전달해야 하는데 대화형은 사용자 턴에 반응하는 방식이라 맞지 않습니다.\n- B: 주제는 이해관계자가 제공하므로 에이전트의 \"발견\" 능력은 요구사항에 없는 과잉 설계입니다.\n- C: 같은 코드 경로를 복제하는 것은 중복 비용입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nAdding configurable inputs to a workflow preserves the workflow class while supporting new topics. The fetch-summarize sequence is still fixed; only the topic input varies. A parameterized workflow is the simplest change that meets the requirement without unjustified architectural escalation.\n(A) Conversational does not deliver scheduled morning summaries. (B) Stakeholders supply the topics; the agent does not need to discover them. (C) Forking the workflow per topic group is unnecessary duplication.","id":"practice-5-2"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent team is asked: under which condition is a conversational system the right architectural fit?","options":["When the team wants to standardize on using Claude's single largest available model for it","When the feature is response-only — no tool use, no state changes, no iteration required","When the team wants to log every user message","When the team wants the feature to take action quickly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념\n- 대화형 = 도구 없음 + 상태 변경 없음 + 반복 없음. 하나라도 필요해지면 워크플로/에이전트 영역입니다.\n- 모델 크기, 로깅, 속도는 아키텍처 클래스를 결정하는 변수가 아닙니다 (직교 관계).\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nConversational systems fit when the feature is purely response-driven: take a user input, return an answer, no external action. FAQ-style answers, definitions, and tone-shaping fit; anything requiring tool use or state changes does not.\n(A) Model choice is independent of architectural class. (C) Logging is achievable in all classes. (D) Taking action quickly suggests external operations, which the conversational class does not perform.","id":"practice-5-3"},{"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following is the MOST common reason to over-engineer a Data Extraction feature as agentic when a workflow would have sufficed?","options":["Treating tool use as the signal for agentic architecture even when the tool sequence is fixed","Choosing the wrong Claude model for the specific task at hand when first setting up the feature","Not configuring any system prompt at all for the feature","Using JSON mode instead of free-form output"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: \"도구를 쓴다 ≠ 에이전트다\"\n- 워크플로도 고정된 순서로 도구를 호출합니다. 에이전트의 기준은 런타임에 다음 행동을 모델이 결정하는가입니다.\n- 시험 함정: \"tool use가 있으니 agentic\" 이라는 선택지는 거의 항상 오답 또는 과잉 설계 패턴입니다.\n\n오답 이유: B(모델 선택), C(시스템 프롬프트 유무), D(출력 형식)는 모두 아키텍처 클래스와 직교하는 요소입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nConflating tool use with agentic architecture is the most common over-engineering pattern. Workflows commonly call tools in fixed sequences; tool use does not justify agentic complexity. CCA-F principles emphasize that the discriminator is runtime decision-making, not tool presence.","id":"practice-5-4"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research team has been told that their report-generation system needs to support both daily fixed reports and ad-hoc deep-dive analyses. Which architectural pattern MOST cleanly satisfies both requirements?","options":["A single agent that handles both the daily fixed reports and the open-ended ad-hoc deep dives together","A workflow for the daily fixed reports and an agent for ad-hoc deep dives, with a router between them","A single workflow that handles both daily reports and deep dives","Two separate products with no shared infrastructure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 하이브리드 = Router + (Workflow | Agent)\n- 기능별로 적합한 클래스를 배정하고, 라우팅·로깅·오케스트레이션 인프라는 공유합니다.\n- 하나의 클래스로 합치면 (A) 고정 업무에 불필요한 비용·분산이 생기고 (C) 개방형 업무를 처리하지 못합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nDifferent requirements warrant different classes. Daily fixed reports fit a workflow; ad-hoc deep dives fit an agent. The router classifies the request type and dispatches accordingly — CCA-F's canonical hybrid pattern.\n(A) A single agent inherits agentic variance and cost on the daily fixed reports. (C) Deep dives require runtime adaptation that a workflow's fixed sequence cannot provide. (D) Full separation duplicates infrastructure without benefit.","id":"practice-5-5"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Data Extraction agent is tasked to extract all line items from quarterly filings and store them as audited records. Midway, a required source API returns a permanent schema-change error. The agent replans, finds a looser scraping path that succeeds, and completes, but the records now skip the mandatory audit validation. An architect reviewing the incident must identify the core replanning defect. What is the MOST accurate diagnosis?","options":["The agent should never replan after a tool error; it should halt immediately and escalate every failure to a human reviewer","The replan satisfied the immediate obstacle but was not validated against the original goal constraint requiring audited records","The agent misclassified the error; a permanent schema-change error should have been retried with exponential backoff until it cleared","The agent used in-context memory for the audit records when it should have used semantic memory for similarity recall"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 재계획은 \"원래 목표 제약\"에 대해 검증되어야 한다\n1. 에러 분류: transient(재시도·백오프) vs permanent(재시도 무의미, 에스컬레이션/대안).\n2. 재계획 후 반드시 원래 목표의 불변 제약(audit validation 등)을 충족하는지 재확인.\n3. 비협상 제약은 프롬프트가 아니라 프로그램적으로 강제(훅/게이트) 해서 어떤 replan도 우회하지 못하게 합니다.\n4. 영구 오류는 우회하지 말고 사람에게 에스컬레이션합니다.\n\n오답 이유: A는 과잉 반응(모든 실패를 사람에게), C는 영구 오류에 백오프를 쓰는 오분류, D는 메모리 종류와 무관한 문제입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nReplanning must be checked against original goal constraints; here the new plan resolved the API failure but silently dropped the mandatory audit-validation requirement. A non-negotiable constraint like audit validation should also be programmatically enforced so no replan can bypass it, and a permanent error should escalate rather than be routed around.\n(A) Blanket halting destroys the adaptivity that justifies an agent. (C) Exponential backoff is for transient errors; retrying a permanent schema change never succeeds. (D) Memory type is irrelevant to the failure.","id":"practice-5-6"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research orchestrator is asked to decompose a task with both research and writing concerns. Which decomposition shape is MOST consistent with CCA-F guidance?","options":["One single combined subagent that performs both the research work and all of the writing together within just one single combined call","Separate subagents for research and writing because they require different capabilities and operate on different inputs","Many small subagents each handling one paragraph","One subagent doing research and the orchestrator doing all writing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 분해의 축은 \"단계/관심사(concern)\"이지 출력 단위(문단)가 아닙니다. 오케스트레이터는 조율·핸드오프만 맡고 실행은 서브에이전트가 합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nResearch and writing are distinct concerns with different inputs (sources versus structured findings) and different capabilities (search versus generation). CCA-F guidance favors decomposition by concern when capabilities differ — each subagent specializes, and the orchestrator handles the handoff between them.\n(A) Combining distinct concerns loses specialization. (C) Per-paragraph subagents over-decompose. (D) The orchestrator's role is coordination, not direct content production.","id":"practice-5-7"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent team has a decomposition that produces correct outcomes but with high latency. Which decomposition adjustment MOST likely improves latency without sacrificing correctness?","options":["Reduce the model temperature to make subagents faster","Combine every one of the separate subtasks together into one single very large subtask instead","Identify independent subtasks and run them in parallel rather than sequentially","Use a smaller model for slow subagents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 지연(latency)의 구조적 레버는 의존성 없는 서브태스크의 병렬 실행입니다. 온도·모델 교체는 구조적 해법이 아니고, 서브태스크 병합은 정확성(중간 검증)을 해칩니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nLatency improves when independent subtasks run in parallel rather than sequentially. CCA-F guidance distinguishes truly independent subtasks (parallel) from dependent ones (sequential); identifying parallel-eligible subtasks is the standard latency optimization.\n(A) Temperature affects sampling, not wall-clock time. (B) One large subtask loses controllability and recovery benefits. (D) Smaller models can produce lower-quality output that triggers retries — net latency may not improve.","id":"practice-5-8"},{"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following BEST captures why CCA-F guidance favors task decomposition for complex Code Generation work?","options":["Decomposition controls scope per step, enables recovery from partial failure, and supports targeted validation","Decomposition mainly makes the overall system look considerably more impressive and sophisticated in the architecture diagrams shown to leadership","Decomposition reduces the number of Claude API calls required","Decomposition makes the system run faster regardless of dependency structure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념 (3대 이점): ① 단계별 범위 통제 ② 부분 실패 복구 ③ 의미 있는 경계에서의 검증. 호출 수 감소나 무조건적 속도 향상은 분해의 이유가 아닙니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nDecomposition's value comes from three properties: scoped per-step control, recovery from partial failure, and validation at meaningful boundaries.\n(B) Aesthetic impressiveness is not a CCA-F design driver. (C) Decomposition typically increases the number of API calls. (D) Decomposition helps latency only when dependencies allow parallelism.","id":"practice-5-9"},{"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following BEST describes the role of ambiguity handling in a Multi-Agent Research orchestrator?","options":["Always proceed without asking, because asking interrupts the user's flow","Detect when the orchestrator's task is under-specified and either clarify, route to a default with disclosure, or escalate","Always refuse ambiguous tasks because ambiguity is inherently unsafe","Ambiguity handling is the user's responsibility rather than the system's, since the user authored the under-specified request and can restate it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 모호성 대응 3가지 표준 응답\n1. Clarify – 사용자에게 되묻기\n2. Default with disclosure – 합리적 기본값으로 진행하되 가정을 공개\n3. Escalate – 사람/상위로 에스컬레이션\n\n안티패턴: 조용한 추측(silent guess), 무조건 거부, 사용자에게 책임 전가.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nAmbiguity handling at the orchestrator covers detection, response, and escalation. CCA-F guidance favors three canonical responses: clarification, default-with-disclosure, or escalation — depending on context. Silent guessing is the anti-pattern.\n(A) Always-proceed is the silent-guess anti-pattern. (C) Always-refuse is over-strict. (D) Pushing all ambiguity handling to the user produces poor UX.","id":"practice-5-10"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research platform team is documenting decomposition guidelines for the entire org. The team wants a small set of properties that uniformly apply across feature types. Which set MOST cleanly captures CCA-F guidance?","options":["Always use the maximum decomposition the orchestrator can manage","Decompose the work strictly by team ownership, by the total file count, by the line count, and by the overall number of API calls that get made each run","Decompose by natural concerns, right-size each subtask, make dependencies explicit, scope to downstream needs, and design boundaries for validation","Always use one giant subtask to avoid coordination overhead"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념 — 분해 5속성 (암기용)\n\n# — 속성 — 의미\n1 — Natural concerns — 자연스러운 관심사 경계로 분해\n2 — Right-size — 과소/과다 분해 방지\n3 — Explicit dependencies — 의존성 명시 → 올바른 순서/병렬 결정\n4 — Scope to downstream needs — 다음 단계가 필요한 만큼만 전달\n5 — Validation boundaries — 검증 가능한 경계 설계 → 복구 지원\n\n오답 이유: A(최대 분해)는 조율 비용 폭증, D(단일 거대 태스크)는 통제·복구·검증 상실, B(팀 소유권·파일 수 등)는 CCA-F의 설계 축이 아닙니다. 시험은 \"균형\"을 묻습니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nThe five properties cover the CCA-F decomposition design space: concerns drive natural boundaries; right-sizing addresses over/under-decomposition; explicit dependencies enable correct sequencing; scoping prevents waste; validation-aligned boundaries support recovery. The set uniformly applies across feature types.","id":"practice-5-11"},{"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following Data Extraction scenarios MOST clearly requires explicit ambiguity handling?","options":["An extraction agent encounters a document with an ambiguous field name that could match two different schema fields","An extraction agent finds all required fields cleanly mapped but should still run ambiguity handling defensively on every document","An extraction agent that has been upgraded to the latest Claude model","An extraction agent that is simply running at a high temperature"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 모호성은 입력의 속성입니다. 해석 선택이 출력 값을 바꿀 때에만 명시적 처리(되묻기, 검증, 해석 로깅)가 필요합니다. 모델 버전·온도는 모호성을 만들거나 없애지 않습니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nField-name ambiguity is a case where the agent's interpretation directly affects extracted values. CCA-F guidance favors explicit ambiguity handling — either clarification, validation, or recording the interpretation — when interpretation choice changes outputs.\n(B) Clean mapping is the absence of ambiguity. (C) Model version does not by itself create or resolve ambiguity. (D) Temperature affects sampling variance, not the presence of ambiguity.","id":"practice-5-12"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Data Extraction agent processes batches over multiple sessions. Which BEST describes when external memory is the right fit per CCA-F guidance?","options":["When state must persist beyond a single session or grow beyond what fits in the context window","Whenever the team happens to have database infrastructure available, since the presence of a database is what makes external memory the natural choice","Only when the system is running on the largest Claude model","Never, because in-context memory handles all of the state"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 메모리 선택 프레임워크\n- 외부 메모리: 세션을 넘어 지속(durability)되거나 컨텍스트 창을 넘는 규모(scale)일 때.\n- 컨텍스트 내 메모리: 단일 세션의 소규모 상태 (검색 오버헤드 없음).\n- 인프라 보유 여부·모델 크기는 선택 기준이 아닙니다. 요구(내구성/규모)에 맞춰 선택합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nExternal memory fits when durability (cross-session) or scale (beyond context window) is required. CCA-F guidance favors external memory for these properties; in-context cannot satisfy them.\n(B) Infrastructure availability is not the design driver. (C) Model choice is independent of memory choice. (D) In-context cannot handle durable or large state.","id":"practice-5-13"},{"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following BEST captures CCA-F guidance on naming subagents?","options":["Names should be sequential, like agent-1 and agent-2, to avoid biasing the model with role-laden labels, since neutral identifiers keep the agents interchangeable","Names should be generic so the subagents can be reused across scopes","Names do not matter at all as long as the underlying code works","Names should reflect the subagent's scoped responsibility, not generic capability — 'refund-processor' rather than 'helper'"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 이름은 설계 명확성의 일부입니다. `refund-processor`, `ticket-classifier`처럼 범위(scope)를 드러내는 이름이 좋고, `agent-1`·`helper` 같은 일반/순번 이름은 책임 정보를 잃게 합니다. 재사용성은 이름의 일반성이 아니라 능력 기반 설계에서 나옵니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nScope-reflecting names make the orchestrator-subagent model's structure explicit. CCA-F guidance favors names that describe responsibility — 'refund-processor', 'ticket-classifier' — over generic names like 'helper' or 'agent' which obscure scope.","id":"practice-5-14"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Code Generation team's programmatic guardrail blocks dangerous operations but produces unclear error messages, frustrating the agent and users. Which CCA-F-consistent design improvement MOST directly applies?","options":["Make guardrail error messages informative — explain what was blocked, why, and what alternative actions are permitted","Remove the guardrail in order to eliminate the error messages","Make the guardrail silent so that it blocks the action without telling the agent, since hiding the block keeps the agent from trying to reason its way around the rule","Just increase the overall strictness of the guardrail across the board"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 좋은 차단 메시지 = What + Why + Alternatives\n- 차단 사실, 이유, 허용되는 대안을 알려 줘야 에이전트가 허용 범위 안에서 재계획할 수 있습니다.\n- 조용한 차단(silent block)은 에이전트가 같은 행동을 반복하거나 혼란에 빠지게 합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nUnclear guardrail errors produce frustration and ineffective agent recovery. CCA-F guidance favors informative error messages that explain the block and suggest alternatives. The agent can then replan within the allowed scope rather than retrying the blocked action.\n(B) Removing the guardrail eliminates the protection. (C) Silent blocks leave the agent confused about what failed. (D) Increased strictness worsens user frustration without improving message clarity.","id":"practice-5-15"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Code Generation team uses PreToolUse hooks for input validation. The team finds that some inputs that should be blocked pass through, while some that should pass are blocked. Which CCA-F-consistent diagnosis MOST directly applies?","options":["Just remove the hook and accept all of the incoming inputs as-is","Hooks are inherently inaccurate and cannot be trusted","Hook validation logic is miscalibrated — refine matching rules against ground-truth examples of both should-pass and should-block inputs","Block all of the inputs in order to prevent any false negatives, since rejecting everything guarantees no malicious input ever slips past the hook undetected"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 오탐(false positive)과 미탐(false negative)이 동시에 있으면 규칙 보정 문제입니다. should-pass / should-block 정답 세트로 규칙을 다듬고 양방향 균형을 맞춥니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nMiscalibrated hooks produce both false positives (over-block) and false negatives (under-block). CCA-F guidance favors calibrating hook logic against ground-truth examples — known should-pass and should-block inputs — to refine matching rules toward correctness.\n(A) The fix is calibration, not removal. (B) Hooks are as accurate as their logic. (D) Blocking all inputs maximizes false positives.","id":"practice-5-16"},{"domain":1,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent team has documented a customer-onboarding workflow in prose but the agent occasionally skips steps. Which CCA-F-consistent design improvement MOST directly applies?","options":["Strengthen the prose instructions with much more detail, since a more thorough natural-language description will reliably get the agent to follow the workflow steps","Define the workflow as code — explicit sequence with programmatic enforcement of step ordering, not just prose documentation","Just trust the agent to follow the workflow eventually on its own","Simply remove the workflow requirement from the system altogether"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 프롬프트 기반 vs 프로그램적 강제\n- 산문 지시는 확률적 → 단계 누락 가능. 순서가 중요한 규정은 코드(상태 머신/게이트 전환/검증된 상태) 로 강제합니다.\n- Q14(audit validation 강제), Q2·Q43(훅/가드레일)과 같은 맥락: \"중요한 규칙은 프로그램으로 강제\".\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nProse-documented workflows are probabilistic — the agent may skip steps under varied inputs. CCA-F guidance favors workflow-as-code with programmatic enforcement: explicit sequence, gated transitions, and validated state. The workflow is a structure, not just documentation.","id":"practice-5-17"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent orchestrator has 12 tools today. The team wants to know the most important signal that they're approaching the partitioning threshold. Which observable signal BEST indicates impending degradation?","options":["The raw number of tools exceeds ten, which the team adopts as a hard rule so that partitioning is triggered by a simple count anyone can check without inspecting traces","Total tokens spent on the tool definitions exceeds five thousand, which the team can measure precisely and treat as the clean numeric tripwire for when the catalog has grown too large to route reliably","The orchestrator starts making wrong tool selections, redundant calls (calling the same tool twice for the same intent), or routing-drift across similar queries — all observable in traces","The orchestrator's average end-to-end response time increases, since a slower agent is the most visible symptom that the growing tool catalog has started to overwhelm the single routing step"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 도구 수 과부하의 \"경험적\" 신호\n\n신호 — 의미\nWrong selection — 잘못된 도구 선택\nRedundant calls — 같은 의도에 같은 도구 중복 호출 (어느 것이 맞는지 구분 못 함)\nRouting drift — 비슷한 질문에 매번 다른 도구 선택\n\n- 파티셔닝(도구 카탈로그 분할) 시점은 트레이스에서 관찰되는 선택 품질 저하로 판단합니다. \"10개 초과\", \"토큰 5천 초과\" 같은 고정 수치 규칙은 오답 패턴입니다.\n- 지연(latency)은 느린 도구, 큐잉 등 다른 요인이 많아 진단력이 낮습니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nThe empirical signals of tool-count overload are wrong selections, redundant calls (the model picks the same tool twice because it can't tell which is right), and routing drift (similar queries produce different selections). All are observable in traces. When these signals appear, the orchestrator's tool catalog has outgrown reliable single-agent selection and partitioning becomes the right move.\n(A) No hard rule exists at 10 tools — the threshold is empirical and depends on tool overlap. (B) Token cost is related but not the primary signal — selection quality degrades before token cost becomes a problem. (D) Latency is affected by other factors (slow tools, queueing).","id":"practice-5-18"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Code Generation team designs a `run_command` tool that takes shell-like arguments. They want to constrain what Claude can pass without rewriting the tool for every new project. Which schema design BEST balances flexibility with safety?","options":["Take a base64-encoded command string for transport safety, then decode and execute it, so the wire-level payload stays clean while the model still controls the command","Take a single freeform `command_string` parameter and let the model construct any shell command it judges appropriate, since a flexible string adapts to every new project without a schema change and trusts the model to pick safe commands","Take an array of arguments and join them with spaces, accepting whatever the model passes","Use a discriminated-union schema with a `command_type` enum and per-type argument shapes (e.g., type=test → test_runner enum + test_path; type=build → build_target enum), validated server-side against an allowlist for each type"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: Discriminated union + enum + 서버측 allowlist\n```json\n{ \"command_type\": \"test\",  \"test_runner\": \"pytest|jest\", \"test_path\": \"...\" }\n{ \"command_type\": \"build\", \"build_target\": \"web|api\" }\n```\n- 안전 경계는 \"문법(표면 형태)\"이 아니라 허용된 명령 집합(allowlist) 에 있어야 합니다.\n- 새 프로젝트는 새 `command_type`을 추가하는 방식으로 확장 → 유연성 유지.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nDiscriminated unions with per-type argument shapes give Claude a constrained, well-typed selection space for each command class. Each type has its own allowlist of acceptable arguments, validated server-side. This balances flexibility (new command types can be added) with safety (no escape into arbitrary shell). It's the standard pattern for safe parameterized command execution.\n(A) Encoding for transport does nothing for execution-time safety. (B) Freeform command strings are functionally arbitrary code execution — the canonical antipattern. (C) Array-of-args still permits arbitrary shell semantics — the safety boundary needs to be in the allowed command set, not the surface syntax.","id":"practice-5-19"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent orchestrator dispatches refund work to a refund-subagent. The refund-subagent has access to the refund API tool. The orchestrator also has the refund API tool registered to handle emergency direct refunds. What concrete cost does this duplication impose?","options":["The orchestrator's tool registry consumes roughly twice the memory due to the duplicate tool definitions being held in both the orchestrator's and the subagent's registries simultaneously throughout the session's lifetime","Duplicate registrations cause the MCP protocol to reject the session at startup","The subagent's calls will be routed to the orchestrator's tool handler instead of its own","The orchestrator may invoke the refund tool directly for routine cases, bypassing the audit logging and review steps the refund-subagent layer adds — meaning compliance-relevant context is lost"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 도구 스코프는 서로 겹치지 않게(disjoint) 유지\n- 서브에이전트가 감사 로깅/자격 검증/리뷰로 도구를 감싸고 있다면, 오케스트레이터가 같은 원본 도구를 갖는 순간 그 통제를 우회할 수 있습니다.\n- 비용은 성능이 아니라 컴플라이언스·품질 통제의 상실입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nWhen a subagent wraps a tool with additional layers (audit logging, eligibility checks, review), duplicating the underlying tool at the orchestrator level lets the orchestrator skip those layers. The duplication's concrete cost is the loss of the wrapper's compliance and quality controls on every direct-call path. Partitioning only works when the orchestrator cannot skip the subagent.\n(A) Memory cost is negligible. (B) Tools are scoped per agent — no session-level conflict. (C) Each agent's tools are dispatched to its own handlers.","id":"practice-5-20"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research tool accepts an `embedding_model` parameter as a free-text string. Claude passes valid model names roughly 80% of the time but occasionally passes outdated or non-existent ones, causing failures. The team maintains a list of supported models. Which schema change BEST eliminates the invalid-model calls?","options":["Accept any string for embedding_model and have the server reject invalid or outdated names with a clear error message, so the agent learns from the failure and the supported-models list stays enforced at the server boundary","Add a description note that lists all the supported embedding models, so Claude can read the note and pick a currently valid name from it on each call","Type the parameter as an enum with the current supported model names, regenerated from the supported-models list whenever the list changes","Pin the parameter to one default model and remove the model choice entirely, so the agent can never pass an invalid name because it no longer selects the model at all"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: \"제약을 왼쪽으로(shift left)\"\n- 유효 값이 유한하고 목록이 관리되고 있다면 → enum으로 스키마에 박고, 목록 변경 시 스키마를 자동 재생성합니다.\n- 강제력 순서: 스키마 enum(작성 시점 차단) > 서버 거부(호출 낭비) > 설명 문구(준수 의존).\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nEnums constrain the parameter to a closed set of valid values at the schema layer, and Claude treats the enum values as the allowed options. Regenerating the enum from the team's supported-models list whenever it changes keeps the schema in sync with reality. The validator also rejects non-enum values as a hard backstop. This is the canonical pattern for closed-set parameters.\n(A) Server-side rejection burns a tool call; move the constraint left. (B) Description notes are weaker than schema-level enums. (D) Removing the choice loses the agent's legitimate ability to pick models.","id":"practice-5-21"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research team's MCP server exposes 30 tools. The team wants to keep the server maintainable as the tool set grows. Which architectural practice BEST supports long-term maintenance?","options":["Limit the server to 10 tools and create additional servers for additional tools","Put all 30 tool definitions in a single file so the server has one source of truth","Have each tool implement its own logging, error formatting, and input validation independently for maximum flexibility, so every domain team can tune its tools' behavior without being constrained by a shared handler's conventions","Group tools into modules by domain (search, analysis, export) and register each module's tools through a shared handler with consistent error semantics"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 도메인별 모듈 + 공통 핸들러(에러 의미, 로깅, 검증 일관성). 임의의 도구 수 상한, 단일 파일, 도구별 독립 구현은 모두 유지보수 함정입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nModular grouping with shared handler conventions keeps a growing tool set comprehensible: per-domain modules group related tools, and shared handler scaffolding ensures consistent error semantics, logging, and validation across tools. This scales to dozens of tools without per-tool boilerplate drift.\n(A) A fixed 10-tool cap is arbitrary and pushes complexity into multi-server orchestration. (B) A single file scales poorly past about a dozen tools — review burden, merge conflicts, inconsistency creep. (C) Per-tool independent implementations produce drift in error messages, log fields, and validation strictness. Shared handler scaffolding is the antidote.","id":"practice-5-22"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent's host application uses an MCP client to connect to a server. The team asks how the client surfaces server-exposed prompts to the user. Which behavior is the typical pattern?","options":["Prompts cannot be surfaced to the user — they are server-internal only","Prompts are surfaced to the model on every turn so the model can use them autonomously, treating each prompt as just another capability it may invoke whenever it judges the template relevant to the current task","Prompts are emailed to the user when the session starts, so the user receives the catalog out of band and selects from it later","The host application renders prompts in a UI surface like a slash menu or command palette, letting the user pick a prompt and supply its arguments before the prompt's content enters the conversation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: MCP 3대 프리미티브의 제어 주체\n\nPrimitive — 제어 주체 — 예\nTools — Model (모델이 호출 결정) — ticket-lookup\nResources — Application (앱이 주입 결정) — 계정 레코드\nPrompts — User (사용자가 선택) — 정중한 답변 템플릿, `/` 메뉴\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nPrompts in MCP are user-driven primitives, and the standard pattern is for the host application to render them in a UI affordance — slash menu, command palette, sidebar — where the user can browse, pick, and parameterize. Once invoked, the prompt's content (often a template with the user's arguments) enters the conversation as a turn. This places initiation with the user, which is the whole reason prompts exist as a distinct primitive.\n(A) Prompts are designed for user-facing exposure. (B) Auto-surfacing to the model collapses prompts into tool-like semantics. (C) There is no email mechanism in MCP.","id":"practice-5-23"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A customer support platform uses MCP: the AI calls a ticket-lookup function, the app injects the account record into each session, and the user selects a formal-reply style template. A new engineer asks why three primitives are needed instead of one universal capability type. What is the BEST architectural justification?","options":["Using three primitives reduces the number of JSON Schema definitions the server must maintain for each exposed capability.","Each primitive has a distinct controller: model, application, or user. Merging them would blur accountability and make access-scoping impossible to enforce.","Separating primitives allows the server to cache tool results, resource data, and prompt templates independently for better performance.","Three primitives let the client rotate credentials separately for each capability type during an active session."],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 프리미티브 분리 = 제어 평면(control plane)과 책임 소재의 분리 → 접근 범위 설정(access scoping), 권한·주입 정책, 감사가 각각 가능해집니다. 성능·스키마 수·자격 증명 회전은 이유가 아닙니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nThe three-primitive model encodes ownership. Merging primitives would make it impossible to apply different authorization and injection policies to model-invoked actions, host-injected data, and user-chosen templates.\n(A) The distinction is about control planes and ownership, not schema count. (C) Caching is an implementation detail. (D) Credential rotation is not the rationale.","id":"practice-5-24"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Developer Productivity team's MCP server tool sometimes wants to return very rich content — formatted Markdown plus a code block plus an image. Which return mechanism BEST supports this?","options":["Concatenate everything into one large text string with inline references to the image, so the whole rich response travels as a single block the model can read top to bottom without juggling separate items in the result's content array at all on its way back","Return multiple content items in the tool result's content array — a text item with the Markdown, a separate text item with the code block, and an image item with the binary — the client passes the array to the model intact","Make three separate tool calls, one returning the Markdown, one returning the code block, and one returning the image, so each content type comes back cleanly typed in its own dedicated response","Rich content is not supported in MCP tool responses at all, so the tool has to flatten everything down to plain text and drop the image, returning only what the protocol's text-only result shape can carry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 도구 결과는 `content` 배열이며 `text`, `image`, `resource` 등 타입이 있는 항목을 여러 개 담을 수 있습니다. 하나의 호출로 혼합 콘텐츠를 반환합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nMCP tool result content arrays support multiple typed items per response — text, image, resource references. Returning multiple items lets a single tool produce mixed-type rich content. The client passes the array to the model, which sees all items in order. This is the protocol's mechanism for rich tool returns.\n(A) Single-string concatenation loses the structured shape. (C) Multiple tool calls multiply roundtrips. (D) MCP supports rich content via the content array.","id":"practice-5-25"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A CI/CD Integration MCP server exposes a `run_shell` tool. A reviewer flags it as too permissive. Which hardening change is MOST important before this server ships to production?","options":["Add a description noting that the agent should only use safe commands","Replace the open shell with an allowlist of permitted commands and validated argument shapes, enforced server-side before any execution","Log every shell command for post-hoc review so operators can audit exactly what the agent executed and catch any dangerous invocations after the session completes","Require user confirmation before each shell invocation in the client UI, so a human reviews and approves every command the agent proposes to run, putting a person in the loop on the riskiest possible tool"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 예방(Preventive) > 탐지(Detective) > 안내(Advisory)\n- 서버측 allowlist(예방) → 로깅(탐지) / 사용자 확인(보완) → 설명 문구(권고, 프롬프트 인젝션 시 무력).\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nArbitrary shell execution is the canonical example of a too-broad MCP capability. The mitigation is an allowlist enforced server-side: only specific commands with specific argument shapes are permitted, and every invocation is validated against the list before execution. This is the difference between a shippable production tool and an arbitrary RCE primitive.\n(A) Description-level guidance does not constrain what the tool can actually execute. (C) Logging is a detective control; allowlisting prevents, logging only records. (D) User confirmation can complement allowlisting but does not replace it.","id":"practice-5-26"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Developer Productivity team uses Claude's built-in tools (web_search, code-execution) alongside a custom MCP-exposed company-knowledge tool. They want consistent observability — every tool call should appear in their tracing system. Where is observability integration BEST implemented?","options":["At the host application layer — wrap every tool dispatch (built-in or MCP) with the same tracing instrumentation, so all calls flow through one observability point regardless of tool origin","Integrate observability only inside the MCP tools, since Claude's built-in tools already produce their own tracing and do not need the team to add any further instrumentation to appear in the tracing system","Inside the model — instruct the agent to log a trace event before and after every tool call","On the network layer — log all HTTP requests from the agent and reconstruct traces from that"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 관측성은 호스트 애플리케이션의 tool-dispatch 계층에 구현합니다 (결정론적이고 모든 도구 출처를 포괄). 모델이 로그를 남기게 하는 방식은 비신뢰적이고, 네트워크 계층은 STDIO를 놓칩니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nThe host application is the layer that dispatches both built-in and MCP tool calls, so it's the natural integration point for unified tracing. Wrapping at the dispatch layer means every call gets the same tracing fields, the same trace IDs, and the same correlation logic.\n(B) Built-in tools may produce some logs but not the team's tracing fields or correlation. (C) Model-emitted tracing is unreliable. (D) Network-layer reconstruction misses STDIO MCP calls (no network traffic).","id":"practice-5-27"},{"domain":2,"stem":"A Developer Productivity team wants to ship an MCP server that runs on each developer's local machine and is launched as a child process by the IDE. Which MCP transport is the BEST fit?","options":["gRPC transport — high-performance RPC with protocol buffers, giving the IDE a strongly typed, low-latency binary channel to the locally running server process","StreamableHTTP transport — the IDE connects to a localhost HTTP endpoint on a configured port, since a stable network socket gives the editor a cleaner reconnect story than a subprocess whose stdin and stdout it must manage","WebSocket transport — bidirectional message stream over a long-lived connection","STDIO transport — the IDE launches the server as a subprocess and communicates over its standard input and output streams"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: MCP 전송 방식은 2가지\n\nTransport — 적합한 경우\nSTDIO — 호스트가 서브프로세스로 띄우는 로컬 서버 (네트워크 노출·인증 불필요)\nStreamableHTTP — 네트워크로 호스팅되는 원격/공유 서버\n\ngRPC, WebSocket은 MCP 표준 전송이 아닙니다 (JSON-RPC over STDIO 또는 StreamableHTTP).\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nSTDIO transport is the canonical choice for locally-hosted MCP servers launched as subprocesses by the host application. There is no network exposure, no authentication required, and process lifecycle is naturally tied to the host. This is the default fit for desktop and IDE integrations.\n(A) gRPC is not an MCP transport. (B) StreamableHTTP works on localhost but introduces port management, lifecycle decoupling, and network attack surface. (C) WebSocket is not one of MCP's supported transports.","id":"practice-5-28"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Developer Productivity team accepts the /init-generated CLAUDE.md without changes. Which characterization of /init output BEST informs whether to ship it as-is?","options":["It is auto-updated by Claude Code on every commit, so any manual edits a team makes are wasted effort that the next scaffold pass will silently overwrite","It is exam-validated and ready for team-wide rollout without modification","It is a starting point — teams should review and refine before adopting it as the project standard","It is encrypted and cannot be edited safely — teams must regenerate to make changes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: `/init` = 초안(scaffold). 팀 규칙 추가 → 잡음 제거 → 문장 다듬기 → 버전 관리에 커밋. 자동 갱신/암호화/검증 완료 같은 선택지는 모두 사실이 아닙니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\n/init produces a scaffold based on automatic repository analysis. It is intentionally a draft: teams need to add conventions, remove noise, and tighten language before relying on it as the project standard. Treating the generated draft as a finished standard skips the review that makes it trustworthy.\n(A) /init does not auto-update on commits. (B) /init has no exam-validation step. (D) CLAUDE.md is plain Markdown and is meant to be edited.\nOverall: /init generates a scaffold CLAUDE.md, not a final team standard. The expected workflow is to refine it for the team's conventions and check it into source control. Shipping it as-is is a tested anti-pattern.","id":"practice-5-29"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent maintainer asks whether starting a new Claude Code session in the same repo will recall context from the previous session. What is the correct CCA-F answer?","options":["Yes — but only if the user keeps the terminal window open between sessions","Yes — Claude Code maintains a rolling persistent memory across sessions for each repository, so reopening the same repo restores the prior conversation automatically","No — Claude Code has no persistent cross-session memory by default; only CLAUDE.md and initial prompts carry forward","Yes — the Claude API caches conversational state under each repo's path for 24 hours"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 세션 간 지속되는 입력은 CLAUDE.md + 초기 프롬프트뿐입니다. 이전 작업이 필요하면 CLAUDE.md에 문서화하거나 프롬프트에 다시 제공해야 합니다. 프롬프트 캐싱 ≠ 세션 메모리.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nClaude Code sessions are bounded by their own context window. Across sessions, the only durable inputs are CLAUDE.md content and what the user provides in initial prompts. Conversational state from a prior session does not persist automatically.\n(D) Prompt caching applies to repeated prompt prefixes within a short window; it is not a 24-hour repo-keyed conversational memory. Conflating cache behavior with session memory is a known trap.","id":"practice-5-30"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A CI/CD Integration pipeline launches Claude Code to triage a failing test. The job logs show Claude reading the failing test, then the test source, then the production module under test, then the recent git diff. Which characterization BEST describes what is happening?","options":["Claude is running a hardcoded developer-authored script that fetches the files in a fixed order, since any sequence the developer can predict in advance is a deterministic script rather than runtime reasoning","Claude is fetching files via a router workflow that classifies the failure type first","Claude is running parallel Reads as a fan-out and the logs are merely interleaved","Claude is iterating its agentic loop — selecting each next Read based on observations from prior tool results"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 각 Read의 대상이 이전 결과에 의존한다 = 에이전틱 루프(perceive → plan → act → observe). 고정 스크립트(워크플로)·라우터·병렬 fan-out과의 구분 기준은 \"다음 행동을 런타임 관찰로 결정하는가\"입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nClaude Code runs a perceive-plan-act-observe loop. Each Read result updates context, and the next tool call is selected dynamically against the goal of triaging the test failure. The investigative chain is exactly that loop in action.\n(A) A fixed-order fetch would be a deterministic workflow. (B) A router workflow has a fixed branching topology decided up front. (C) The sequence is causal; fan-out requires independent subtasks.","id":"practice-5-31"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A CI/CD Integration job uses Claude Code to attempt a refactor. Halfway through, Claude detects that a precondition is unmet and stops without making changes. Which characterization of the execution model BEST fits the stop behavior?","options":["The -p flag forces stop at the first uncertain step regardless of task state","Claude Code crashed silently and the user should restart the session immediately","Only hooks can stop the loop, so without a registered hook Claude must always continue running until it produces a final answer no matter how impossible the precondition makes the task","The agentic loop can terminate at any iteration when Claude judges progress impossible — early stops are part of the model, not a failure"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 루프 종료 3가지 — ① 작업 완료 ② 오류 ③ 진행 불가 판단. 조기 중단은 잘못된 변경을 커밋하지 않게 막는 설계의 일부이므로 \"충돌\"로 오진하면 안 됩니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nClaude Code's loop terminates on task completion, on error, or on an explicit decision that progress is not possible. Stopping when a precondition fails is exactly the third — a deliberate termination by the model, not a bug or hook fault.\n(A) -p controls interactivity, not uncertainty thresholds. (B) A deliberate halt with an explanatory message is not a crash. (C) Hooks are one stopping mechanism but not the only one.","id":"practice-5-32"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Code Generation team wants to know what /compact does inside Claude Code. Which description is MOST accurate?","options":["It permanently archives the session to disk for later replay","It summarizes earlier turns in the current session to free context while preserving the thread","It disables CLAUDE.md mid-session to reduce token usage by roughly 50%, trading away project rules for a smaller context footprint","It moves the accumulated session state to a configured MCP server for offloaded storage, freeing the local context window by relocating history to the external service"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: `/compact` = 세션 내 요약 압축 (손실 있음, 긴 세션용). CLAUDE.md(상시 설정)는 건드리지 않고 대화 이력만 줄입니다. 보관·MCP 오프로드 기능이 아닙니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\n/compact is the built-in command for in-session compaction. It replaces older verbose turns with a summary, freeing context to continue the session. The thread is preserved but at lower fidelity for older content.\n(A) Not an archive-to-disk command. (C) /compact does not disable CLAUDE.md. (D) /compact has no MCP involvement.","id":"practice-5-33"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Code Generation user wants to start a session with a precise problem statement plus three reference files already in context. Which approach BEST achieves that under Claude Code's session model?","options":["Use the Claude API directly with file attachments, because Claude Code itself cannot ingest local files into a running session and must defer to the raw API for any content loading","Embed the entire content of all three files inside CLAUDE.md so every session preloads them","Open the session, ask Claude to Read the three files, and then state the problem — the loop loads them on demand","Configure a PreToolUse hook to inject the three files before each tool call"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: CLAUDE.md = 규칙(상시), Read = 일회성 콘텐츠(온디맨드). 일회성 자료를 CLAUDE.md에 넣으면 모든 세션 컨텍스트가 부풀어 오릅니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nClaude Code's loop ingests files via Read when asked. Issuing explicit Read calls at the top of the session ensures the three reference files are in context before Claude reasons about the problem.\n(A) Claude Code ingests files via Read at any point. (B) Inlining files into CLAUDE.md inflates every session's context. (D) Hooks are not a clean way to seed initial context.\nOverall: CLAUDE.md is for rules, not for one-off context; the Read tool is for content.","id":"practice-5-34"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Data Extraction team's project-level CLAUDE.md grew bloated as multiple owners added rules. The team is debating splitting it across files. Which split-strategy is MOST aligned with the hierarchy model?","options":["Encode the split as SKILL.md files instead of CLAUDE.md sections","Distribute the rules across several CLAUDE.md files all at the repository root and rely on a documented load order to combine them in sequence","Move some rules to user-level CLAUDE.md to lighten the project file","Use subdirectory CLAUDE.md files for path-specific rules and keep root CLAUDE.md focused on global conventions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 스코프 보존 분할\n- 루트 CLAUDE.md = 전체 공통 규칙 / 하위 디렉터리 CLAUDE.md = 해당 경로 전용 규칙.\n- 사용자 레벨로 옮기면 스코프·우선순위가 바뀌어 팀 표준에 부적합. 같은 위치에 다중 파일 로드 개념은 없음. Skills는 상시 규칙 대체재가 아님.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nSubdirectory CLAUDE.md naturally scopes rules to the paths they apply to, which removes irrelevant noise from sessions touching only certain folders. The root keeps cross-project rules; subdirectory files own path-specific behavior.\n(A) Skills are not a substitute for standing rules. (B) There is no documented multi-file load at the root; only one CLAUDE.md applies per tier at each location. (C) Moving rules to user-level changes their scope and precedence.","id":"practice-5-35"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent team wants Claude Code's every tool call logged to a central file with timestamp and arguments. Which hook BEST fits?","options":["A PostToolUse hook that logs tool call details to the file and exits 0","A PreToolUse hook that captures the call details and exits with code 2, since blocking is how the hook ensures it sees and records every tool invocation","A Stop hook, since it logs activity neatly at the end of each task and gives one consolidated record rather than scattered per-call entries","Skip hooks entirely, since Claude Code already logs every tool call by default to /var/log/claude with timestamps and arguments included"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 훅 이벤트와 종료 코드\n\n이벤트 — 시점 — 용도\nPreToolUse — 도구 실행 전 — 검증/차단 (exit 2 = 차단)\nPostToolUse — 도구 실행 후 — 로깅/감사 (exit 0 = 계속)\nStop — 작업 종료 시 — 최종 처리 (호출별 로깅 부적합)\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nPostToolUse fires after every tool call with details available. Logging plus exit 0 produces an auditable trail without blocking. This is one of the most common documented hook patterns.\n(B) Exit 2 blocks every tool call. (C) Stop fires at task completion, not per tool call. (D) No such default logging exists; centralized per-call logging has to be built.","id":"practice-5-36"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A CI step running `claude -p` with --dangerously-skip-permissions needs the ANTHROPIC_API_KEY to be available. What is the recommended way to provide this in a CI environment?","options":["Hardcode the API key in the CI pipeline YAML file for simplicity, since CI configurations are typically not public","Store in CI secret management; inject as ANTHROPIC_API_KEY at runtime","Pass the API key as a -p argument: `claude -p 'API_KEY=sk-... Review this code'`","Run `claude login` before the -p step to authenticate interactively and cache credentials for subsequent runs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 비대화형 인증 = 환경 변수 `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` + CI 시크릿 저장소. 하드코딩·인자 전달·대화형 로그인은 모두 오답.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nCI secret management systems (GitHub Actions secrets, GitLab CI variables, AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, etc.) store secrets encrypted, access-controlled, and inject them as environment variables at runtime without appearing in logs or configuration files. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is the environment variable Claude Code reads for authentication.\n(A) YAML files are often committed to version control. (C) Embedding the key in the -p argument exposes it in process lists, CI logs, and shell history. (D) Interactive login is not viable in CI.","id":"practice-5-37"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Developer Productivity team builds an SDK-driven service that runs many short Claude Code sessions to triage incoming tickets. They need each session bounded in duration, the team's project CLAUDE.md and custom slash commands honored, structured JSON returned for downstream routing, and per-session observability. The architect must design how these requirements compose through the SDK. Which design BEST satisfies all of them?","options":["Use the SDK but disable CLAUDE.md so sessions start faster, and reconstruct any needed instructions in the prompt each time","Call the Claude API per ticket with the routing schema in the prompt, since CLAUDE.md and slash commands transfer automatically to API calls and the model loads the project configuration the same way a Claude Code session does","Run each session under the project working directory with a session time limit, request structured output, and instrument the SDK for per-session telemetry","Skip session time limits and rely on ticket volume staying low so sessions naturally finish quickly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: Claude Code SDK ≠ raw Claude API. 프로젝트 작업 디렉터리에서 실행해야 CLAUDE.md/슬래시 명령이 적용되고, 시간 제한·구조화 출력·텔레메트리가 각각 SDK 기능에 매핑됩니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nSDK-driven sessions honor the project's CLAUDE.md and custom slash commands when run in the project context, satisfying configuration inheritance. A session time limit bounds duration, requesting structured output yields routable JSON, and instrumenting the SDK gives the per-session observability needed. Each requirement maps to an SDK capability rather than to the raw model API.\n(A) Disabling CLAUDE.md discards the standing instructions. (B) The Claude API does not load CLAUDE.md or custom slash commands. (D) Hoping volume stays low is not a duration bound.","id":"practice-5-38"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research team wants -p runs to read CLAUDE.md from a specific path, not the working directory's default. Which characterization BEST fits?","options":["Pass a --claude-md=PATH flag to point the run at a specific file explicitly, since the CLI accepts an option that overrides where it looks for the configuration","Set the working directory to the project root before invoking -p — CLAUDE.md discovery follows the working directory hierarchy","Copy the CLAUDE.md into a hardcoded path that the run always reads from, staging the file there before each invocation so discovery lands on the right one","Accept that -p ignores CLAUDE.md regardless of its location, so the team has no real way to control which configuration a non-interactive run picks up"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: CLAUDE.md 탐색은 위치(작업 디렉터리 계층) 기반. 존재하지 않는 `--claude-md` 플래그는 전형적인 함정 선택지입니다. `-p`도 CLAUDE.md를 읽습니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nClaude Code resolves CLAUDE.md by location. The working directory determines what is in scope. Set the cwd (cd or the runner's working directory) to the desired project root and the hierarchy resolves correctly.\n(A) There is no --claude-md flag. (C) Setting the working directory is cleaner than copying. (D) -p honors CLAUDE.md.","id":"practice-5-39"},{"domain":3,"stem":"A Code Generation team implements a PreToolUse safety hook that blocks Bash commands matching dangerous patterns (`rm -rf`, `DROP TABLE`, `format C:`). A developer notices that the hook is also blocking legitimate commands that happen to contain these substrings. What is the root cause and fix?","options":["The hook is case-sensitive — adding case-insensitive matching would prevent false positives.","The hook should run PostToolUse instead of PreToolUse — post-execution pattern matching avoids blocking legitimate commands.","Substring matching false-positives on legitimate commands — use specific regex with word boundaries","The hook should maintain a whitelist of allowed commands — only listed commands execute, all others are blocked."],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 오탐의 원인은 패턴 특이성 부족. 해결은 PreToolUse를 유지하면서 패턴을 정밀화(단어 경계, 명령 호출 위치 확인). PostToolUse로 바꾸면 위험 명령이 이미 실행된 뒤입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nSubstring matching (e.g., blocking any command containing 'rm -rf') will false-positive on commands like `echo 'Do not run rm -rf'` or `grep 'rm -rf' file.txt`. More precise patterns — regex word boundaries, checking that 'rm -rf' is a command invocation rather than a string argument, or requiring the pattern at the command start — reduce false positives while maintaining safety coverage.\n(A) Case sensitivity is secondary. (B) PostToolUse defeats the safety purpose — the command would already have executed. (D) A strict allowlist is far more restrictive than needed.","id":"practice-5-40"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent's prompt says 'Verify the customer's identity before sharing account details.' The model sometimes shares partial details without verification. Which change BEST hardens the rule?","options":["Lower temperature so the model is less likely to share details prematurely, since a calmer sampler tends to withhold sensitive account data by default","Add 'verify thoroughly and completely' to the existing prompt so the model takes verification seriously","Specify the verification steps — confirm full name plus last 4 of SSN plus billing ZIP — and refuse account details until all three match","Wrap account details in an <account> XML tag in the response"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: \"형용사/부사는 규칙이 아니다\"\n- 모호한 동사(\"verify\")는 모델이 부분 신호로도 충족했다고 해석할 수 있습니다.\n- 해결: 필요 조건을 열거(이름 + SSN 뒤 4자리 + 청구지 ZIP)하고 기본 동작(거부) 을 명시.\n- 온도 조정, 강조 부사, XML 태깅은 규칙을 만들지 못합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\n'Verify identity' is interpretable; the model can treat partial signals as sufficient. Enumerating the three required match conditions and the refusal default until all three match converts the rule into a structural gate the model can enforce reliably.\n(A) Determinism does not impose a verification protocol. The model deterministically shares partial details on the same unstated rule. (B) Adverbs do not introduce rules. (D) Output tagging does not prevent sharing; it only labels what was already shared.\nOverall: Identity verification is a multi-condition rule. Enumerating each required check and the refusal default makes the gate auditable and consistent. This pattern generalizes to any safety-critical conditional behavior — never leave 'verify' as a single verb.","id":"practice-5-41"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research orchestrator prompt says 'Combine subagent findings into a final report.' Final reports omit some findings without explanation. Which change BEST ensures every finding appears?","options":["Lower temperature to reduce the omission variance so the same findings survive each run","Increase max_tokens so all findings have room to fit, since omissions are caused by the model running out of output space on long reports","Require: enumerate every finding ID in an appendix, mark each as included/excluded with a one-sentence rationale","Concatenate all subagent outputs verbatim so nothing can be dropped in synthesis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 커버리지를 \"감사 가능한 구조\"로 — ID별 포함/제외 + 1문장 사유. 막연한 품질 목표를 구조적 요구사항으로 바꾸는 패턴입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nOmission without explanation comes from 'combine' being underspecified. Requiring an ID-by-ID accounting plus rationale for exclusion makes coverage auditable: missing findings are visible, and the model must justify each exclusion rather than dropping them silently.\n(A) The model deterministically omits the same findings. (B) Token capacity is rarely the cause; the model still chooses to omit. (D) Verbatim concatenation eliminates synthesis entirely — that is the orchestrator's job.\nOverall: When synthesis must preserve full coverage, the rule must be explicit: account for every input with included/excluded status and rationale.","id":"practice-5-42"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A CI/CD Integration prompt asks Claude to recommend whether to merge a PR. Recommendations are sometimes 'merge' and sometimes 'consider merging.' Reviewers want a binary decision. Which change BEST enforces a binary verdict?","options":["Add 'be decisive and commit to a single verdict' to the prompt so it stops hedging","Lower temperature to 0 so the recommendation is more confident, since a deterministic verdict reads as decisive and that is what reviewers want","Require the response to end with exactly one of: VERDICT: MERGE or VERDICT: HOLD — with no other terminal phrasing allowed","Use prefilling to start the response with 'I recommend' so it opens decisively"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 허용 값 열거 + 위치 규칙(마지막 줄에 정확히 하나). 파싱 가능한 출력이 되어 CI가 프로그램적으로 처리합니다. Prefill은 시작만 고정할 뿐 종결 판정은 제약하지 못합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nThe hedging comes from the absence of a binary output rule. Specifying an exact terminal phrase from a two-option set converts the recommendation into a parseable binary decision that CI can act on programmatically, with no ambiguity.\n(A) Decisiveness adjective does not enumerate the allowed verdict set. (B) Temperature does not introduce a binary rule. (D) Prefilling anchors the opening but does not constrain the terminal verdict.\nOverall: Binary or n-ary decisions require explicit enumeration of the allowed terminal values plus a placement rule — PR merge verdicts, escalation decisions, sentiment labels.","id":"practice-5-43"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Data Extraction prompt has 11 explicit criteria for field handling but no rule for what to do when a field is missing. Outputs sometimes substitute null, sometimes empty string, sometimes the literal word 'unknown.' Which change MOST directly stabilizes missing-field handling?","options":["Lower temperature so the missing-field choice becomes deterministic, since a stable sampler settles on one consistent marker for absent fields each run","Drop missing fields from the output entirely so no ambiguous marker is needed","Add the 12th criterion: 'Missing fields must use null; never empty string, never sentinel strings'","Use JSON mode so the output is always syntactically valid for parsers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 규칙 세트는 부재(negative) 케이스까지 덮어야 합니다. \"선택한 표현(null) + 금지 대안(빈 문자열, sentinel)\"을 함께 명시. JSON mode는 문법만 보장할 뿐 의미적 선택은 하지 않습니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nEleven criteria leave an obvious gap at the 12th case. Specifying the explicit missing-value representation plus the explicit forbidden alternatives closes the gap. The model now has a complete rule set and downstream parsers receive consistent missing-field markers.\n(A) Determinism on no rule produces deterministic ambiguity per input. (B) Dropping fields breaks downstream consumers expecting a fixed field list. (D) JSON mode accepts null, empty string, and 'unknown' as valid values; it does not pick one.\nOverall: Rule sets must cover the negative case. Specifying both the chosen marker (null) and the forbidden alternatives closes the rule.","id":"practice-5-44"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent prompt instructs Claude to 'apologize when the customer is upset.' Some responses apologize for routine inquiries, others miss apologies after genuine complaints. Which change BEST calibrates the apology behavior?","options":["Lower temperature to make the apology choice deterministic on each run","Always apologize first to be safe, since leading with an apology guarantees no upset customer is ever missed even on routine inquiries and the courtesy rarely offends","Use few-shot with two upset and two non-upset tickets so the model learns the boundary","Specify trigger conditions for apology — explicit complaint, service failure mentioned, frustration phrasing — and one canonical apology sentence"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 감정 단어(\"upset\") 추론 대신 관측 가능한 트리거 조건(명시적 불만, 서비스 실패 언급, 좌절 표현)과 정형 문장을 제공. Q3·Q45와 같은 원리: \"명시적 규칙 > 온도/예시 몇 개/강조\".\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\n'Upset' is a subjective inference. Naming three observable trigger conditions plus a canonical apology phrase gives the model both a clear gate (when to apologize) and a controlled response (how to apologize), eliminating both over- and under-apology.\n(A) The model deterministically follows an unstated rule, producing the same drift. (B) Always-apology becomes performative; the fix is calibration, not maximalism. (C) Four examples cannot span the upset spectrum; edge cases remain inconsistent without a stated trigger rule.\nOverall: Sentiment-driven behaviors need observable trigger conditions, not feeling-word inference — apology, empathy, escalation, congratulations.","id":"practice-5-45"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Data Extraction team has two pipelines using the same Claude model with different system prompts — one for invoices, one for purchase orders. Both prompts are nearly identical except for the schema names. A developer suggests consolidating them into one prompt that handles both via a runtime flag. Which assessment is MOST accurate?","options":["Routing logic must always live in the system prompt for consolidation to work","Consolidation is always better because it reduces maintenance overhead","Two separate user messages on one consolidated prompt achieve the same isolation as two prompts, since distinct turns keep the contexts logically apart","Consolidation increases prompt complexity and risks cross-document interference; keep specialized system prompts unless the deduplication win is large"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 작업별 전문화된 시스템 프롬프트가 범위·예시·엣지 케이스를 격리합니다. 통합은 플래그 분기 오류와 교차 간섭 위험이 있으며, 중복 제거 이득이 클 때만 고려. \"always better\" 같은 극단 표현은 함정.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nSpecialized system prompts isolate each task's scope, examples, and edge cases, preventing one document type's rules from influencing the other. Consolidation forces the model to branch on a flag, which is itself an additional source of routing error. Specialization is the safer architecture unless duplication cost dominates.\n(A) Routing logic in the system prompt is exactly the pattern that introduces interference. (B) Always-statements are red flags. (C) User messages do not isolate system-prompt context.\nOverall: This intersects with D1's routing-workflow pattern: prompt specialization is the prompt-layer analogue of a routing workflow.","id":"practice-5-46"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A CI/CD Integration tool uses few-shot examples to teach Claude to classify build failures into three categories: configuration errors, test failures, and dependency issues. Examples cover all three categories, but Claude misclassifies failures that span two categories. Which example design addition BEST addresses this?","options":["Add one or more examples where a failure spans two categories, showing the correct multi-label output","Add instructions specifying that failures can belong to multiple categories","Add a tiebreaker rule: when in doubt, classify as configuration error to ensure a single deterministic output","Use extended thinking so Claude reasons about ambiguous failures before classifying"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: Few-shot은 출력 형태의 가능 공간을 가르칩니다. 단일 라벨 예시만 주면 항상 하나만 고릅니다 → 경계/모호 사례(다중 라벨)를 예시에 포함하세요.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nMulti-label examples directly demonstrate that outputs can span categories, teaching Claude the correct behavior for ambiguous failures through demonstration rather than instruction alone.\n(B) An instruction helps but is less reliable than a demonstrated example. (C) A default tie-breaking rule masks the multi-label capability. (D) Extended thinking aids reasoning but does not teach the multi-label output pattern.\nOverall: When all examples are single-label, Claude learns to always pick exactly one category. Including examples where a failure genuinely spans two categories teaches the model that multi-label outputs are valid.","id":"practice-5-47"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent's tool-use extraction has a 'priority' enum [low, medium, high]. The business adds 'urgent' as a fourth level. Which migration approach MOST aligned with schema-evolution discipline?","options":["Add 'urgent' to the enum, regenerate the schema, update few-shot examples to demonstrate when urgent applies, and run the test dataset to confirm prior cases still classify correctly","Add the new 'urgent' value to the enum silently and deploy it, since a single additive enum value is low-risk and cannot affect how existing tickets are classified","Keep the three existing levels and use 'high' as a proxy for urgent, since reusing an existing value avoids a schema change, keeps the most pressing tickets flagged, and leaves the consumers that depend on the schema untouched","Lower temperature for accurate urgent classification, since a more deterministic setting helps the model assign the new urgency level correctly across tickets"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 스키마 진화 3층 — ① enum/스키마 확장 ② few-shot 예시 갱신(언제 쓰는지 교육) ③ 테스트 데이터셋으로 회귀 검증. 한 층이라도 빠지면 과소 사용 또는 회귀가 발생합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nEnum extension is a typical evolution: add to enum, update examples to teach when to use, regression-test prior cases. The combination ensures the new value is recognized, used appropriately, and doesn't disrupt prior categorizations. This is the schema-as-code discipline.\n(B) Skips regression testing. (C) Loses semantic resolution. (D) Determinism doesn't introduce new categories.","id":"practice-5-48"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Data Extraction pipeline runs extract, then validate against business rules, then re-prompt on failure. The team adds an MCP tool call inside the validation step that occasionally times out, and they currently treat every validation-step failure identically by re-prompting the model. Extraction quality is fine but the loop wastes calls re-prompting on tool timeouts. Which refinement BEST fixes this?","options":["Remove the validation step's tool call so the loop only ever evaluates the model's extracted content","Re-prompt the model on every validation-step failure since re-prompting eventually resolves all errors, and a fresh generation will eventually clear a tool timeout the same way it clears a content mistake","Distinguish transient tool errors from extraction-content failures, retrying the tool with backoff but re-prompting only on content errors","Lower temperature on the extraction step so the validation tool call stops timing out"],"answer":2,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 실패 유형별 복구 라우팅\n\n실패 유형 — 복구 전략\nTransient tool/infra error (타임아웃) — 동일 도구 재시도 + 지수 백오프\nContent failure (비즈니스 규칙 위반) — 실패 사유를 넣어 모델 재프롬프트\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nA tool timeout is a transient infrastructure error suited to retry with exponential backoff, whereas an extraction that violates business rules is a content failure suited to re-prompting the model. Conflating the two wastes model calls re-prompting on a timeout that no prompt change can fix.\n(A) Dropping the tool removes a legitimate business-rule check. (B) Re-prompting cannot fix a tool timeout. (D) Temperature affects sampling, not the MCP tool's response time.","id":"practice-5-49"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent team's evaluation runs in CI and blocks merge on regression. The team also wants to track gradual drift over weeks. Which architectural approach BEST supports both?","options":["Keep the CI gate (per-commit, fast, hard-fail) and add a separate periodic full-evaluation job (daily or weekly) that captures trend metrics over time","Run the full evaluation only in the CI gate, since a single per-commit run covers both the merge-blocking decision and any longer-term trend the team cares about","Run only the periodic evaluation job, since a daily or weekly full run catches regressions and drift alike without the overhead of a per-commit CI gate","Lower temperature to prevent the gradual drift, since a more deterministic setting keeps the scores steady over weeks and removes the need for trend tracking"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 평가 2중 루프 — 커밋별 CI 게이트(빠름, hard-fail) + 주기적 전체 평가(추세/드리프트). 서로 다른 의사결정 시간 척도를 담당합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nTwo complementary loops: CI gates per-commit on hard regressions (fast feedback, block-on-regression); periodic full-evaluation tracks longer-term trends (drift, slow degradation). They serve different decision timescales. Together they cover acute regressions and gradual drift.\n(B) CI loops are short; longer trends need separate cadence. (C) Loses fast per-commit feedback. (D) Drift can have many causes; monitoring is the answer.","id":"practice-5-50"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Multi-Agent Research synthesizer uses evaluator-optimizer: a generator produces a synthesis, an evaluator scores it, the generator revises. The loop occasionally runs indefinitely. Which change BEST prevents infinite loops?","options":["Use only a quality threshold without any iteration cap, since terminating as soon as quality is met is the cleanest stopping rule and an iteration limit is redundant","Trust the evaluator-optimizer loop to terminate on its own, since a well-designed quality signal will naturally converge and stop the generator from revising endlessly","Lower temperature on the generator, since a more deterministic setting makes each revision change less and brings the loop to a stable output sooner","Set explicit convergence criteria — a quality threshold AND a maximum iteration count — terminate on either condition met"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 종료 조건 = 품질 임계값 OR 최대 반복 횟수 (둘 중 하나 충족 시 종료). 반복 상한은 안전망이며, 상한 도달 시 에스컬레이션/최선 결과 반환을 설계합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nWithout a maximum iteration limit, an evaluator-optimizer loop can run indefinitely whenever the quality threshold is never reached. Explicit convergence criteria, a quality threshold plus a maximum iteration count, bound the loop: either condition met terminates it. Without both, the loop has no guaranteed termination.\n(A) Quality threshold alone may never be reached; iteration cap is the safety net. (B) The infinite-loop failure mode shows it doesn't. (C) Doesn't bound iteration.","id":"practice-5-51"},{"domain":4,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent pipeline extracts order IDs and refund amounts from emails into a JSON object. In testing, roughly 15% of responses omit the 'amount' field or return it as a string instead of a number. The team wants to reduce that error rate without replacing the model. Which retry strategy is most aligned with CCA-F validation-retry principles?","options":["Re-run the identical prompt automatically up to three times and accept whichever response first passes a JSON schema check, logging each failed attempt for offline review.","Feed the specific validation failure reason back into the next prompt and cap retries at a fixed budget, escalating when the budget is exhausted.","Use a second model call to critique the first response, then return the critique to a human for final correction.","Raise the retry budget to unlimited retries so every response eventually passes schema validation."],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 검증-재시도 루프 4단계\n1. Validate (스키마/형식/의미 게이트)\n2. Inject failure reason (구체적 오류를 재시도 프롬프트에 포함 → 자가 교정)\n3. Retry budget (지연·비용 상한)\n4. Escalate (예산 소진 시 에스컬레이션)\n\n입력을 바꾸지 않는 단순 재시도는 새 신호가 없어 같은 오류를 반복합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nThis is the canonical validation-retry loop: (1) validate output against schema, (2) inject the specific failure reason into the retry prompt so the model can self-correct, (3) enforce a retry budget to bound latency and cost, and (4) escalate when the budget is exhausted.\n(A) Retrying without changing the input gives the model the same ambiguous signal. (C) A multi-pass reviewer is a review step, not a retry loop; sending critiques to humans by default defeats automated validation. (D) Unlimited retries remove cost and latency controls.","id":"practice-5-52"},{"domain":5,"stem":"A Data Extraction pipeline sends 80KB of CSV rows plus a 2KB extraction schema to Claude. The schema is appended LAST. Extraction quality is inconsistent and rows in the middle of the CSV are often missed. Which change addresses BOTH issues?","options":["Increase the model's max output tokens to allow longer responses","Place the schema at the start, then chunk the CSV across multiple calls","Wrap each CSV row in XML tags to make boundaries explicit","Run the extraction at temperature 0 to make output deterministic so every CSV row is read in a fixed order and none can be skipped"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: Lost-in-the-middle 대응\n- 긴 입력에서 시작/끝은 주의가 강하고 중간은 약해집니다.\n- 대응: ① 중요한 규칙(스키마)을 앞쪽에 배치 ② 입력을 청크로 분할해 각 행이 호출 내에서 앞/끝에 오도록.\n- max_tokens, XML 태그, temperature는 \"입력 주의 예산\" 문제를 해결하지 못합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nPutting the schema at the prompt start anchors structural rules where attention is strongest. Chunking the CSV addresses the lost-in-the-middle row dropout: rows that would have been mid-context in one giant call become beginning- or end-context in their own smaller calls, restoring per-row attention.\n(A) Output token ceilings cap generation length but do not change input attention. (C) XML wrapping aids parsing but does not solve mid-context attention decay across an 80KB input. (D) Determinism does not improve recall on under-attended input rows.\nOverall: Two D5 levers fix this scenario together: front-loading the schema to anchor structural rules, and chunking input so no single call has rows trapped in the lost-in-the-middle zone. Output controls and structural tags cannot compensate for an attention-budget problem.","id":"practice-5-53"},{"domain":5,"stem":"A Data Extraction script reports 'context window limit exceeded' on roughly 5% of records — typically the longest ones. Which is the BEST short-term mitigation while a more permanent chunking refactor is built?","options":["Detect oversize records and split them into two halves processed independently","Skip records that exceed the window and log them as failed, since dropping the longest five percent preserves throughput while the refactor lands","Retry oversize records with exponential backoff, since the limit errors look transient and spacing the retries out tends to let the record through","Lower max_tokens to make room for the oversized input"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 컨텍스트 초과는 결정론적 오류(재시도해도 동일) → 백오프는 무의미. 단기 완화는 이상치만 분할해 정상 경로는 유지하고 데이터 손실 없이 처리합니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nSplitting just the long records keeps the normal path unchanged while preventing overflow on the 5% tail. It is an incremental fix that buys time for the proper chunking refactor.\n(B) Silently skipping records is data loss — exactly the high-stakes failure pattern D5 reliability teaches architects to avoid. (C) Context overflow is deterministic, not transient. (D) Lowering output budget marginally helps but does not address records that exceed input limits by significant margins.\nOverall: Production D5 mitigation patterns split outlier inputs to keep the bulk path intact, deferring full refactors. The principle is to prevent data loss while bounding context.","id":"practice-5-54"},{"domain":5,"stem":"A CI/CD Integration agent's vulnerability scanner returns 0 issues. The scanner's last update was 18 months ago. Which D5 reliability concern is MOST relevant?","options":["Stale scanner definitions can return false negatives — empty results may reflect outdated knowledge, not absence of issues","Treat 0 issues as conclusive, since vulnerability data is static and an 18-month-old definition set is just as valid as a current one for this purpose","Re-run the scanner with the same definitions to confirm","Lower the scanner's reporting threshold so more candidate issues surface"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: \"없음(absence)\" 주장은 근거 데이터의 신선도/커버리지에 의존\n- 빈 결과가 \"문제 없음\"을 뜻하려면 소스가 최신이고 범위가 충분해야 합니다.\n- 고위험 부재 주장(취약점 0건, 오류 0건)은 소스 신선도 확인이 우선. 같은 정의로 재실행·임계값 조정은 해결책이 아닙니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nA scanner is only as good as its definitions; 18 months of un-updated definitions miss every vulnerability discovered in that window. The empty result reflects the scanner's blind spots more than the codebase's actual safety. CCA-F architects check freshness of the data sources behind absence claims before treating absence as evidence.\n(B) Vulnerability databases evolve daily. (C) Re-running with the same stale definitions returns the same 0; freshness is the gap. (D) Lowering thresholds in stale definitions still cannot detect vulnerabilities the scanner does not know about.\nOverall: Absence claims can reflect source staleness rather than reality. D5 reliability discipline includes checking the freshness and coverage of underlying data sources, especially for high-stakes absence claims like 'no vulnerabilities' or 'no errors.' This composes with provenance tracking and source-of-record checks.","id":"practice-5-55"},{"domain":5,"stem":"A Code Generation agent escalates when a generated patch fails its test suite three times in a row. The team observes the agent's recent failed patches sometimes contain partial progress. Which adjustment to the handoff BEST captures this?","options":["Discard the failed patches and pass only the original task, since a clean restart avoids confusing the reviewer with broken code","Include the most recent failed patch and the test failure log in the structured handoff","Auto-revert the failed patches and re-run silently","Pass only the running count of failed attempts to the reviewer queue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 핸드오프 페이로드 = 다음 행위자가 필요한 것\n- 최근 실패 패치 + 테스트 실패 로그(+ 시도 이력) → 사람이 \"개선/방향 전환/재시작\"을 판단.\n- 시도 횟수만 전달, 작업 폐기, 조용한 자동 복구는 모두 정보 손실 또는 사람 개입 지연.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nA reviewer often needs to see the work-in-progress to decide whether to refine, redirect, or start over. Including the most recent patch and the failure log preserves the agent's progress in the handoff so the human can build on it.\n(A) Discarding the work loses signal about where the agent got stuck. (C) Auto-revert delays human input that the trigger demands. (D) A count without content is not actionable.\nOverall: Escalation handoffs should preserve the agent's progress so humans can build on it rather than restart. The structured payload becomes a bridge from autonomous work to human review, not a hard cut.","id":"practice-5-56"},{"domain":5,"stem":"A Data Extraction agent queries a regulatory database and receives a successful HTTP 200 response with an empty JSON array []. The agent must decide whether to report 'no regulatory filings found' or investigate further. Which approach is MOST correct?","options":["Report 'no regulatory filings found' since HTTP 200 with an empty array is a valid empty result.","Treat the empty array as a possible database error and retry the query.","Escalate to a human reviewer because an empty result from a regulatory database is inherently suspicious.","Query a backup database to confirm the empty result before reporting it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 접근 실패 vs 빈 결과 구분\n\n응답 — 의미 — 처리\nHTTP 200 + `[]` — 조회 성공, 일치 레코드 0 — 권위 있는 빈 결과로 보고\nHTTP 503 / 404 / 타임아웃 — 접근 실패 (모른다) — 에러로 전파/재시도/에스컬레이션\n\n- 과거 5B 퀴즈의 `success / empty_result / access_failure` 상태 구분과 동일한 원칙입니다. 에스컬레이션·교차 검증은 정책 기반이지 결과 내용만으로 자동 발동하지 않습니다.\n- 주의: Q8(스캐너 신선도)과 비교 — Q44는 시스템이 정상 응답한 경우이고, Q8은 소스 자체가 낡아 부재 주장의 근거가 약한 경우입니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nHTTP 200 confirms the database was reachable and executed the query successfully. An empty JSON array is an authoritative empty result: the system is saying there are no matching records. This is the correct distinction from an access failure.\n(B) A successful query with zero results is not an error; retrying wastes resources. (C) Escalating on any empty result would create excessive false escalations; escalation criteria should be policy-defined, not based on result content. (D) Cross-validation should be driven by policy (e.g., high-value entities), not triggered automatically on any empty result.\nOverall: Domain 5B3 requires distinguishing access failures from empty results. HTTP 200 + empty array is a definitive empty result — categorically different from HTTP 503 (unreachable) or HTTP 404. Agents must not treat successful queries returning zero records as errors — they must report them as authoritative empty results.","id":"practice-5-57"},{"domain":5,"stem":"A Data Extraction team's review interface shows reviewers the agent's output with confidence scores but not the input context. Reviewers struggle to evaluate decisions without seeing what the agent saw. Which adjustment BEST fits?","options":["Have reviewers approve or reject based on the confidence scores alone, since a calibrated confidence number already encodes everything a reviewer needs to judge whether the output is trustworthy","Include the agent's input context (or a faithful summary) in the review interface alongside the output and scores","Reduce the confidence threshold so that reviewers are shown noticeably fewer cases to evaluate","Hide the agent's output from reviewers and have them re-derive the correct answer independently from scratch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 리뷰 UI는 입력 컨텍스트 + 출력 + 신뢰도를 함께 보여주는 의사결정 지원 도구여야 합니다. 신뢰도 점수만으로 승인하면 형식적 도장 찍기(rubber-stamping)가 됩니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nReviewers cannot evaluate decisions without seeing the input the agent reasoned over. The review interface must include the input context — the original record, the relevant fields, the supporting documents — so the reviewer can validate the agent's reasoning, not just the output. The structured handoff payload should include this context.\n(A) Confidence-only review is rubber-stamping. (C) Fewer cases routed does not help reviewers evaluate the cases they do see. (D) Hiding the output discards useful information and forces re-derivation.","id":"practice-5-58"},{"domain":5,"stem":"A Customer Support Agent platform routes 10% of cases to human review using random sampling. The team wants to improve calibration quality. Which sampling refinement BEST fits?","options":["Stratify by confidence bucket and customer segment so each stratum is represented proportionally in the review sample","Increase the random sampling rate to thirty percent, since a larger random sample naturally draws in more of every kind of case and therefore improves calibration coverage across the board","Route only Enterprise-tier cases to review, since those are the highest-value segment to get right","Reduce the random sampling rate down to one percent so reviewers have far fewer cases to work through daily"],"answer":0,"explanation":"핵심 개념: 층화 샘플링(Stratified sampling) — 신뢰도 구간 × 고객 세그먼트 같은 중요한 층을 식별해 비례적으로 표본 추출. 표본을 늘리는 것(30%)은 희소 층을 여전히 비례적으로만 반영하고, 줄이는 것(1%)은 더 악화시킵니다.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nRandom sampling reproduces production distribution and may under-represent rare combinations. Stratified sampling by confidence bucket and customer segment ensures each stratum contributes to calibration and quality monitoring. Calibration becomes accurate across all sub-populations, not just the common ones.\n(B) More random sampling still under-represents rare strata proportionally. (C) Single-segment routing misses other segments' calibration signal. (D) Smaller random samples worsen rare-stratum coverage.\nOverall: Stratified sampling outperforms random for calibration quality across sub-populations. Random is a starting point; stratification is the upgrade.","id":"practice-5-59"},{"domain":5,"stem":"A Code Generation agent generates a refactor proposal that says 'matches the pattern used in module X.' Reviewers find the claim is false — module X uses a different pattern. Which provenance practice would have caught this BEFORE review?","options":["Raise the temperature on the agent's pattern claims to encourage more diverse and creative refactor suggestions","Trust the agent's pattern-matching claim without verification, since the agent examined module X while drafting the proposal and is therefore well-positioned to judge whether the patterns truly match","Forbid the agent from making any claims that reference patterns used in other modules of the codebase","Require the agent to cite specific functions or files in module X and validate the citations against actual code"],"answer":3,"explanation":"핵심 개념: Claim-to-source 매핑 + 자동 검증\n1. 주장마다 구체적 출처(함수·파일) 요구\n2. 코드로 검증: 인용한 대상이 존재하는가? 실제로 일치하는가?\n3. 리뷰어의 경계심에 의존하지 않고 리뷰 전에 차단.\n\n[공식 해설 원문]\nVague 'matches the pattern' claims are unverifiable and frequently hallucinated. Requiring specific citations and validating them catches the failure before review. The validation can be automated (does the cited function exist? does it actually match?) closing the gap with code rather than relying on reviewer vigilance.\n(A) Temperature is unrelated to verifiability. (B) Trust without verification is the failure mode. (C) Forbidding cross-module claims removes useful refactoring signal.\nOverall: Vague claims invite hallucination; specific citations enable validation. CCA-F architects require the agent to be specific about sources and then automate the validation step.","id":"practice-5-60"}]};
